1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:09,767
Antigravity Swarm: you think three separate files cost you three waits, but actually three agents code them at once, and usually another tests them before you look.

2
00:00:10,267 --> 00:00:11,292
Here's what changed.

3
00:00:11,592 --> 00:00:17,640
Before, one agent took users, orders and products in turn; after each, you ran the tests yourself.

4
00:00:18,140 --> 00:00:31,023
Now, in preview, you type /swarm, and your chat becomes the coordinator: it gives each file its own worker, at the same time, plus, usually, a verifier that runs the tests and sends problems back to the coder.

5
00:00:31,523 --> 00:00:35,702
They all talk on one shared board you can watch: questions, test results.

6
00:00:36,202 --> 00:00:41,232
The catch: it works best when a task splits cleanly, and it uses your quota much faster.

7
00:00:41,732 --> 00:00:50,696
So, what we learned: three files used to mean three waits; now, when the task splits, three agents take them at once, and usually a verifier tests first.

8
00:00:51,196 --> 00:00:51,898
Want more?

9
00:00:52,198 --> 00:00:54,729
Subscribe, and comment what we should explain next.
